WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.560
Hi, my name is Mateo Robles.

2
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.000
I'm a computer science graduate who builds useful

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.320
software and brings clarity to complex work.

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.060
I'm ready to contribute, learn quickly, and take

5
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.560
ownership. In the GitHits design partnership, I helped

6
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.800
connect an AI coding tool to a service

7
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.060
that finds relevant code examples.

8
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.500
The goal is simple: give the tool useful

9
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.040
references while keeping clear limits on what it

10
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.900
can request. My contribution included building that connection,

11
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.220
adding safeguards, and keeping a record of what

12
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.600
happened so the results could be checked.

13
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.480
I tested successful searches, requests that should be

14
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.380
blocked, and whether the records could be trusted.

15
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.720
The demonstration uses controlled test data, not customer

16
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.260
results. Working through review feedback strengthened both the

17
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.660
implementation and how I explained my decisions.

18
00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.560
That is experience solving a real software problem

19
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.460
and making the work clear enough for another

20
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.020
developer to check. I work from first principles:

21
00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.100
start with the real goal and question assumptions.

22
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.200
Then I choose the highest-impact next step, build

23
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.200
the smallest useful version, check the evidence, and

24
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.620
improve it. High agency means taking initiative and

25
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.060
following through. My path from sociology to computer

26
00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.120
science gave me two lenses: how people respond

27
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.600
to incentives, and how systems respond to rules.

28
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.020
Organizing AI tools that work on their own

29
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.560
reminds me of coordinating people: clear roles, shared

30
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.120
goals, context, and good handoffs matter.

31
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.160
I look for both the human and technical

32
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.240
reasons work gets stuck.

33
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.760
Gyre turns mathematics into live browser art.

34
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.180
RISE makes reading interactive through text, audio, and

35
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.820
visuals. I helped the team make readings play

36
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.260
reliably and look consistent.

37
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.640
I'm open to software engineering, technical support, product

38
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.300
or associate product management, and implementation roles.

39
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.980
If you want someone curious, adaptable, and ready

40
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.460
to contribute, invite me to interview.

41
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.500
Let's build something useful together.
